{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_multi_sink_logging", "label": "Multi-sink logging", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_multisinklogger", "label": "MultiSinkLogger", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Fans every record out to several destinations at once, each with its own minimum level. What a hand-written Logger does not get you is the part that is easy to get wrong: the record is built once rather than once per destination, a broken destination stays broken alone, delivery is bounded, and shutdown flushes what is still buffered."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_logsink", "label": "LogSink", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "name, minLevel and write are all that is required; the optional members are attach, flush and close."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_batchingsink", "label": "BatchingSink", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The base class every queueing sink extends, so the delivery settings mean the same thing everywhere: batch size, flush interval, queue capacity, overflow policy, retry count and backoff with jitter."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_sinkdeliveryerror", "label": "SinkDeliveryError", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "How a sink signals worth-retrying from not. A retryable failure backs off exponentially with jitter honouring a server-supplied delay; a non-retryable one is dropped at once, because five retries with backoff only delay the moment somebody notices. An error that is not a SinkDeliveryError counts as retryable - that is what a socket reset or a failed fetch looks like, and those do pass on their own."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_sinkreporter", "label": "SinkReporter", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A sink never logs through the framework logger, because it is the framework logger - reporting a failure that way feeds the failure back into the thing that failed. SinkReporter rate-limits to the console instead."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_bounded_delivery_queue", "label": "Bounded delivery queue", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "When the queue is full a record is dropped - newest by default, oldest with drop-head - the count is kept on the sink, and the loss is reported at most once a minute. An unbounded buffer does not save the records; it converts some logs were lost into the process died, and hides the problem until it is fatal."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_transform_hook", "label": "Redaction transform hook", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Applied once before fan-out, so every destination sees the same redacted record and there is no question of which one got the raw value. Keep it cheap and total - it runs on the caller stack for every record past the level gate - and a transform that throws is reported with the record kept unchanged, because silently deleting somebody log stream is the worse failure."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_per_sink_levels", "label": "Per-sink minimum levels", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Two gates that compose in one direction only: the system level decides what is produced at all, and a sink minLevel narrows it further. A sink asking for debug while the system level is info receives nothing - lower the system level first, then narrow per sink, which is what keeps a suppressed log call as cheap as a comparison."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_shutdown_flush", "label": "Shutdown flush", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "terminate flushes and closes the sinks before whenTerminated resolves, bounded by a close timeout. The flush runs after every actor postStop so a parting message is still in the batch being drained, a destination that hangs cannot hold the process, and records logged after the sinks close fall back to a plain console line - shutdown is exactly when a last message matters."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_close_drains_without_retries", "label": "Close drains with retries switched off", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Whoever is closing already holds a deadline, so a shutdown gets one attempt per batch rather than a backoff schedule that would outlive the process."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_sink_precedence", "label": "Logger configuration precedence", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Code and config do not merge: withLogger or withLogSinks replaces the whole sinks block rather than adding to it. A destination is described in one place or the other, never half in each - and options are validated in the constructor that owns them, which is only possible if that constructor sees all of them. Every HOCON sink ships disabled, so a system with nothing enabled behaves exactly as it did before this existed."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_sink_write_contract", "label": "Sink write contract", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "write never throws (the pipeline catches anyway, but a sink that leans on that loses the rest of its batch) and never blocks (hand the record to a queue and return); anything that batches or retries extends BatchingSink rather than building that machinery again."}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_console_sink_formats", "label": "ConsoleSink text and json formats", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "text is what ConsoleLogger has always written down to the byte, with positional arguments passed through untouched so an Error still renders with its stack; json is what JsonLogger writes down to the key order, so whatever parses your logs today keeps working."}], "edges": [{"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_multi_sink_logging", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_multisinklogger", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_multi_sink_logging", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_logsink", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_multi_sink_logging", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_batchingsink", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_multi_sink_logging", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_sinkdeliveryerror", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/observability/logging/multi-sink.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_observability_logging_multi_sink_multi_sink_logging", 